  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nA departing upper-level shortwave ridge is in the process of\nbeing replaced by a trough and deepening low pressure system \nmaking its way into the central CONUS. The low itself is expected \nto lift northeastward today from the Southern High Plains to the \nUpper Midwest which should direct most forcing north and west of \nour forecast area, but a spoke of the shortwave energy pivoting \noverhead during the afternoon and evening shows at least modest \nheight falls over the ArkLaMiss Delta region. Without prolonged \nwarm, moist advection in advance of this system's cold frontal \npassage, forecast instability is in the range of 500-1500 J/kg \nover northeast LA during the daylight hours tomorrow but \ndecreasing to around 400-700 J/kg as the front moves further east \ninto Mississippi after dark. There is a modest 40kts of deep-layer\nshear expected across the front, but less favorable instability \nsuggests only low potential for pre-frontal convection to develop \ninto discreet supercells beyond northeast LA in the afternoon \nhours. The primary hazards will be wind squalls with line segments\ninto the early morning hours and heavy rainfall rates. Weak \ntornadoes cannot be ruled out. Heaviest rain rates could pose an \nisolated flash flood threat in low-lying or urban areas, but the \nprogressive movement of the front should limit an organized \nflooding threat. The Marginal severe storm risk remains in place\nfor approximately the northwestern half of the area, and the \n\"Limited\" flash flood threat graphic continues to highlight about\nthe northwestern third. \n\nThis weather should clear by Wednesday, but showers could linger in \nthe wake of the front across eastern Mississippi Wednesday morning. \nThe pattern will reinforce mostly dry weather Wednesday into early \nnext week. Building high pressure over the weekend will push \ntemperatures below normal across our forecast area. High \ntemperatures Saturday through next Monday should be mostly in the \nmid to upper 60s or near 70 degrees, while overnight low from Friday \nnight onwards will dip into the 30s and 40s. Frost seems likely in \nthat time frame with any periods of particularly calm overnight \nwinds and mostly clear skies. Will continue to monitor trends, but \nwe are in the time of year where frost and light freeze are normal \nand the growing season has ended already. /NF/86/",
